  "summary": "Compressed air, switched by an air valve, alternately pressurises the air side of two flexible diaphragms joined by a shaft. The pressurised diaphragm pushes liquid out of its chamber through the upper (outlet) ball check, while the other diaphragm is pulled inward, drawing liquid in through its lower (inlet) ball check. At the end of each stroke a pilot shifts the air valve and the cycle reverses. Discharge pressure cannot exceed air supply pressure (1:1 ratio pumps), so the pump simply stalls against a closed valve.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Positive displacement – reciprocating",
    "configuration": "Double diaphragm, pneumatically driven, mechanically connected diaphragms",
    "valves": "Ball check valves (flap valves are an alternative for large solids)"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "diaphragm": "Moulded elastomer with convolution (PTFE overlay versions exist)",
    "materials_shown": "Aluminium wetted castings, stainless plates/seats, PTFE balls, polymer centre section",
    "stroke_display": "±12 mm (animated); real stroke is size- and design-dependent",
    "cycle_note": "Air valve and pilot arrangements vary by manufacturer; the spool shown is a generic representation."
  },
